            <ArticleTitle>Diagnosing acute aortic dissection with aneurysmal degeneration with point of care ultrasound.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1384.e3-1384.e4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0735-6757(17)30434-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2017.05.052</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a case with visual images of an aortic dissection with aneurysmal degeneration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A case report was described of a patient presenting to the emergency department with back pain. An aortic dissection with aneurysmal degeneration was rapidly identified with bedside ultrasound in the emergency department.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">An aortic dissection was detected rapidly by emergency physician preformed bedside ultrasound. This led to rapid identification and treatment CONCLUSION: Rapid bedside emergency ultrasound can quickly and accurately diagnosis an aortic dissection and lead to rapid treatment.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2017. Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
